Mind’s I / Omnidirectional Self-Model

Source / carrier event

The initiating observation was that closing the eyes does not require imagination to inherit the eyes’ forward-facing field of view. A person may represent the space behind, above, below, and within the body by combining proprioception, memory, symbols, expectation, and uncertainty. The decisive linguistic transformation was:

> Do not limit the internal model to a *mind’s eye*. Treat it as the *mind’s I*.

The pun is functional rather than decorative: it changes the modeled primitive from an internal camera to the situated self that can query and render information through any available modality.

Canonical definition

The Mind’s I is a viewpoint-independent, multimodal, uncertainty-bearing model of self-in-world. It maintains an addressable field around and within the body while rendering only the resolution and modality required by the present goal.

It need not construct a continuously photorealistic panorama. Its default representation may be a sparse embodied scene graph containing locations, relations, possible sensations, affordances, provenance, and confidence. Attention temporarily renders selected regions at higher resolution.

```math
I_t = (P_t, W_t, S_t, M_t, U_t, A_t)
```

Where:

• P_t is the estimated pose and boundary of the self.
• W_t is the spatial or relational world-model.
• S_t is the available sensorium and its current weighting.
• M_t is memory relevant to reconstruction.
• U_t is the uncertainty field.
• A_t is the movable attention cursor.

For an address x in modeled space:

```math
W_t(x) = (contents, relations, affordances, modality, confidence, provenance)
```

A local experience is produced by a rendering request:

```math
R_t = Render(I_t, x, modality, resolution, goal)
```

Resolution operator

The established Adaptive Resolution Mechanism is extended into embodied space:

```math
Z(O, goal, evidence, uncertainty, cost, risk) \rightarrow resolution
```

The Mind’s I applies Z independently across location, modality, time horizon, and semantic depth. Resolution is therefore a field rather than one global setting:

```math
\rho = \rho(x, modality, time, goal)
```

Canonical loop:

```text
Scan → Detect → Select → Zoom → Resolve → Test → Store → Re-scan
```

Canonical law:

> Scan wide. Lock narrow. Test hard. Store layered. Reopen when context changes.

Provenance and epistemic states

Every rendered element must retain one or more source labels:

• sensed — supported by current sensory contact;
• remembered — reconstructed from prior contact;
• inferred — derived from other observations or constraints;
• imagined — deliberately generated as a possibility;
• unknown — retained without premature completion.

Confidence and vividness remain separate variables:

```math
truth_weight \neq vividness
```

The Salience Firewall applies:

```text
salience → inspect
evidence → believe provisionally
prospective result → promote or demote
```

Invariants

1. The self is not the camera. The self-model can position a camera, change modalities, or operate without visual rendering.
2. Unrendered is not absent. Low-resolution or symbolic regions remain part of the model.
3. Representation is not perception, and neither guarantees reality. Provenance and uncertainty travel with every ThoughtObject.
4. Resolution follows purpose. Expensive detail is allocated according to goal, evidence, uncertainty, cost, and risk.
5. The sensorium is plural. Vision, hearing, touch, proprioception, interoception, memory, language, and counterfactual imagination are alternative carriers.
6. The world may remain stable while the I moves. Allocentric modeling is distinct from rotating a forward-facing picture.
7. Every projection is testable in principle. Recontact with reality produces an echo and a delta.

